import{_ as o,o as t,c as r,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BM5YPQmL.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Security posture","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"policies/security-posture.md","filePath":"policies/security-posture.md","lastUpdated":1793736494000}'),a={name:"policies/security-posture.md"};function n(d,e,i,c,l,h){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="security-posture" tabindex="-1">Security posture <a class="header-anchor" href="#security-posture" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Security posture&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The rules the shell keeps when it runs untrusted code next to the user&#39;s identity. Each rule has a scenario in <code>packages/*/features/</code> or a check in <code>scripts/</code>; a rule with neither is a wish.</p><h2 id="mount-rules" tabindex="-1">Mount rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#mount-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mount rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong><code>srcdoc</code>, never <code>src</code>.</strong> The applet document is the verified bytes with the shell prelude and CSP injected outside the signed content. A <code>src</code> URL would give the applet an origin and a network path the shell does not mediate.</li><li><strong><code>sandbox=&quot;allow-scripts&quot;</code> only.</strong> <code>mountApplet</code> sets the attribute to exactly <code>allow-scripts</code>, replacing any tokens the host set. Never <code>allow-same-origin</code>, never <code>allow-top-navigation</code>, never <code>allow-popups</code>. The frame has an opaque origin and cannot reach the shell&#39;s storage or DOM.</li><li><strong>The guard&#39;s CSP, unchanged by profile.</strong> <code>createAppletCsp(profile)</code> is applied through <code>prepareNappletSrcdoc</code>, and <code>planMount</code> appends its <code>trustedTypes</code> directives (<code>require-trusted-types-for &#39;script&#39;; trusted-types default</code>) to the CSP meta kehto rendered; it returns the same policy for every profile. No grant, profile or setting alters it. See <a href="./../concepts/guard#why-csp-never-loosens">Guard</a>.</li><li><strong>No controllers.</strong> On the attached, still empty frame, before any applet document exists, <code>mountApplet</code> calls kehto&#39;s <code>denyNappletControllerAccess(frame)</code>, which rewrites the frame&#39;s <code>allow</code> attribute (its Permissions Policy) to end in <code>gamepad &#39;none&#39;; hid &#39;none&#39;; usb &#39;none&#39;</code> and drops any entry the host had set for those three features. A frame without <code>getAttribute</code> gets the same value as <code>CONTROLLER_DENY_POLICY</code>. This rule is about game controllers and USB or HID devices only. Workers, service workers included, are refused by the CSP&#39;s <code>worker-src &#39;none&#39;</code> (rule 3), not by this policy.</li><li><strong>Order: attached empty frame → harden → register → set environment → observe frame → assign <code>srcdoc</code>.</strong> The host attaches the iframe to the document first, with no <code>src</code> or <code>srcdoc</code> (<code>mountApplet</code> throws <code>invalid:frame-detached</code> without a <code>contentWindow</code>). Then <code>mountApplet</code> sets <code>sandbox</code> and the controller policy, <code>originRegistry.register(contentWindow, windowId, identity)</code>, then <code>originRegistry.setEnvironment(contentWindow, environment)</code>, then <code>bridge.observeFrame(frame, windowId)</code>, then <code>frame.srcdoc = plan.srcdoc</code>. Assigning <code>srcdoc</code> first would let the document&#39;s first message arrive before the shell knows who it is.</li><li><strong>The host page forbids frame navigation.</strong> The HTML the server sends carries <code>KERNL_FRAME_POLICY_META</code>, kehto&#39;s <code>&lt;meta http-equiv=&quot;Content-Security-Policy&quot; content=&quot;frame-src &#39;none&#39;&quot;&gt;</code>, in its <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> before any script runs, as <code>apps/web/src/pages/app/index.astro</code> does. Applet frames still load, because their bytes arrive as <code>srcdoc</code>; a frame that tries to load or navigate to a URL is blocked, so an applet cannot replace its verified document before kehto sees its first load. kehto warns in the console when the policy is missing. See <a href="./../how-tos/embed-kernl-core">Embed kernl core</a>.</li><li><strong>Remove the iframe on every revocation reason.</strong> <code>window-message</code>, <code>second-hello</code>, <code>invalid-hello</code>, <code>second-load</code>, <code>port-closed</code> and <code>host</code> all end with the frame gone and the guard&#39;s state for the window forgotten. A revoked window&#39;s identity is never re-bound to the same frame.</li><li><strong>No frame in the applet&#39;s markup.</strong> <code>planMount</code> calls <code>assertAppletHasNoFrame</code> on the verified bytes before they become a <code>srcdoc</code>, and refuses markup containing an <code>iframe</code>, <code>frame</code>, <code>frameset</code>, <code>object</code>, <code>embed</code>, <code>portal</code> or <code>fencedframe</code> element with <code>denied:nested-frame</code>. It parses with the host&#39;s <code>DOMParser</code> when there is one and otherwise matches lexically, which may over-reject (<a href="./../decisions#d-014">D-014</a>).</li><li><strong>The frame lock runs before applet code.</strong> <code>planMount</code> inserts <code>renderFrameLockScript()</code> immediately after kehto&#39;s namespace prelude. It installs the Trusted Types <code>default</code> policy, which refuses frame markup at every HTML string sink, and pins <code>createElement</code>, <code>createElementNS</code> and <code>createDocument</code> so they throw <code>denied:nested-frame</code> for a frame tag. Because frames can also be minted without a factory, the same script pins every DOM insertion method to refuse a node that is or contains a frame element, refuses custom elements that extend frames, and removes <code>XSLTProcessor</code>; a frame never becomes connected, so it never gets a realm. When kehto&#39;s prepared head is not where <code>planMount</code> expects it, the mount is refused with <code>unavailable:frame-lock</code> rather than run without the lock. This is a port of kehto&#39;s unreleased <code>nestedContextLock</code>, removed when <code>@kehto/shell</code> ships it (<a href="./../decisions#d-014">D-014</a>).</li></ol><h2 id="identity-rules" tabindex="-1">Identity rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#identity-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Identity rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol start="10"><li><strong>Verified bytes come from <code>@kehto/nip/5d</code> <code>resolveNapplet</code>.</strong> Gateways and caches are accelerators; the hash is the identity. Cached bytes are re-verified on every read.</li><li><strong>Identity is <code>(dTag, artifactHash)</code> plus one port.</strong> Grants are keyed by that identity; a new build is a new identity with no grants.</li><li><strong>Restrictive by narrowing.</strong> <code>resolveEnvironment</code> returns only granted domains; kehto intersects, so the hook can only remove. The runtime&#39;s permissive default is never reached for an ungranted domain.</li></ol><h2 id="key-rules" tabindex="-1">Key rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#key-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Key rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol start="13"><li><strong>Keys live only inside applesauce signer instances.</strong> <code>ExtensionSigner</code>, <code>NostrConnectSigner</code>, <code>PasswordAccount</code>&#39;s wrapped signer. kernl never holds raw key bytes and never serialises a private key outside <code>PasswordAccount</code>.</li><li><strong>The shell never gives an applet <code>window.nostr</code>.</strong> kehto&#39;s prelude installs <code>window.napplet</code> and nothing that signs; the shell does not forward the page&#39;s signer, and every signature an applet gets goes through rule 15. kernl does not control browser extensions: one that injects scripts into every frame can still reach into an applet&#39;s frame and expose its own signing object there, which no rule on this page governs. The guarantee is about what the shell provides, not about what the user&#39;s extensions add.</li><li><strong>Destructive signatures wait for the user.</strong> An applet can ask for a signature only through a granted domain. When an applet window asks the shell to sign a destructive kind (every replaceable kind: 0, 3 and 10000–19999; every addressable kind, 30000–39999; a deletion, kind 5; a request to vanish, kind 62), <code>@kernl/core</code>&#39;s consent gate looks up the version it replaces, in the event store and then over the outbox and discovery relays for up to <code>CURRENT_VERSION_TIMEOUT_MS</code> (4 s), shows the change as a semantic diff through <code>host.presentConsent</code> and waits. When no current version is found, the summary says so first (&quot;Current version: could not be loaded; this replaces it entirely&quot;) rather than reading as a harmless first version; a denial fails with <code>denied:consent</code> and nothing is signed or published. Both routes pass the gate: <code>relay.publish</code> through the signer kernl returns from <code>ShellAdapter.auth.getSigner(windowId)</code>, and <code>outbox.publish</code> in kernl&#39;s outbox service wrapper, because kehto&#39;s outbox service signs without naming the window. The shell&#39;s own signing, with no window, is not gated. Routine kinds are signed without a prompt, and answers are not remembered. kehto&#39;s runtime has no such step; this rule is kernl&#39;s.</li></ol><h2 id="network-rules" tabindex="-1">Network rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#network-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Network rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol start="16"><li><strong>No applet network path exists outside the shell.</strong> All bytes arrive through the <code>resource</code> domain and a bridge. An applet frame has no network of its own, and cannot make a child frame that would have one: kehto&#39;s prelude pins every <code>RTC*</code> global (and the <code>webkit</code>/<code>moz</code> variants) to <code>undefined</code>, read-only and non-configurable; rules 8 and 9 stop the applet from creating a child realm where those globals still work; and the CSP refuses <code>fetch</code>, WebSocket, images, frame loads and beacons. <code>tests/e2e/applet-network-sealed.spec.ts</code> proves this in a real browser: 15 frame-creation routes throw, no child realm and no peer-connection constructor exists, and no <code>srflx</code> or <code>relay</code> candidate is gathered. When the host page can reach a public STUN server, the spec asserts that it does and that the applet produces no such candidate; otherwise that control is skipped. The HTTP bridge enforces <code>https</code> only, 10 MiB, 20 s and a private-address block over IPv4 and fully parsed IPv6 literals (<a href="./../concepts/bridges#the-http-bridge">Bridges</a>). In Node and on servers it follows up to 3 redirects by hand and re-checks every hop; in a browser a redirect is opaque to it and is refused with <code>denied:redirect-unverifiable</code>. Hostnames are checked as literals only: no resolver is wired, so a public name that resolves to a private address is not caught. Browser fetches are also subject to CORS: a request the browser blocks is <code>unavailable:network Failed to fetch</code>, and any other status-0 answer is <code>unavailable:network &lt;type&gt; response</code>.</li><li><strong>One <code>RelayPool</code>.</strong> No service, provider or bridge opens its own connection. Adapters over the shared pool satisfy kehto&#39;s contracts.</li><li><strong>Hidden windows are watched, not paused.</strong> The visibility gate holds only a hidden window&#39;s window-level <code>hello</code>. Port traffic is observed just before kehto dispatches it, and the observation cannot hold it (<a href="./../decisions#d-012">D-012</a>): a hidden window&#39;s activity is recorded and its overruns of the unfocused budget are reported, while its <code>resource.*</code> request past the hidden allowance quarantines it, and that request is not dispatched.</li></ol><h2 id="log-rules" tabindex="-1">Log rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#log-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Log rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol start="19"><li><strong>Secrets never appear in logs, guard events or consent summaries.</strong> <code>GuardEvent.reason</code> is a stable prefixed string; <code>NappletRevocation.detail</code> never includes message payloads; <code>describeChange</code> output names fields, not key material.</li><li><strong>Guard events are shown to the user</strong>, with the applet and the reason. They are not sent anywhere.</li></ol><h2 id="dependency-rules" tabindex="-1">Dependency rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#dependency-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dependency rules&quot;">​</a></h2><ol start="21"><li><strong><code>nostr-tools</code> is never imported by kernl source.</strong> It is a direct peer dependency of <code>@kernl/core</code>, because kehto and applesauce require it, but no file under <code>packages/*/src</code> imports it.</li><li><strong>Published packages have zero framework dependencies.</strong> <code>svelte</code>, <code>react</code>, <code>vue</code>, <code>preact</code>, <code>solid-js</code> and <code>lit</code> are forbidden in every <code>packages/*/package.json</code>.</li><li><strong>Leaf packages do not import <code>@kehto/*</code></strong>, except <code>@kernl/guard</code>, which imports <code>@kehto/firewall</code>. Only <code>@kernl/core</code> composes kehto.</li></ol><p>Rules 21 to 23 are enforced by <code>scripts/check-dependency-rules.mjs</code> (<code>pnpm check:deps</code>). Rules 1 to 20 are scenarios in <code>packages/*/features/</code> (the root <code>features/support/</code> only wires their step definitions into vitest), and the mount rules are additionally exercised in the browser by the Playwright suite.</p><h2 id="reporting" tabindex="-1">Reporting <a class="header-anchor" href="#reporting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reporting&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A posture violation is a bug of the highest severity in this repository, above any feature. Report it to the maintainers with the rule number; the source repository is private during the alpha. If it involves a kehto seam, the issue is filed upstream too, and <code>DECISIONS.md</code> records the gap.</p>',17)])])}const g=o(a,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
